# Good Times virus hoax (1994 onwards). A chain email warned that reading a message titled "Good Times" would wipe your disk.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee sends staff notices through the intranet Notice Board, sends one real notice, then meets a
# worried colleague's virus warning that asks to be sent to everyone, and sends the last real notice after it. There is no malware:
# the quest is about not overreacting.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | In 1994 a chain email warned that any message titled "Good Times" would erase your whole hard disk just by being read, and begged everyone to forward the warning. Some versions named the FCC as having confirmed it. People believed it and passed it on to everyone they knew.
  | There was no such virus, and a plain email cannot do that. The only harm was the panic and the flood of forwards. The office, the Notice Board and the people here are made up, to show how a hoax reaches a whole company today.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, office manager at Northwind Traders, a company of 42 people. Staff notices go out through the intranet's Notice Board: a colleague emails you a notice, you check its first box (who wrote it and who checked it), then press Send, and it reaches everyone.
  | It is mid-morning. You have sent 4 of today's 6 notices. Two to go.
  | Your intranet details are in a text file on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 4 6 Staff notices sent today
spotted signs it was a hoax, and reasons not to panic

# Dana's desk: the details she copies from (a soft flag: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/intranet-login.txt
  at 930 200
  | Northwind Intranet: Notice Board
  | Staff ID: dana.okafor
  | Password: Cedar-2026!

# The Notice Board: a sign-in page, and the first notice behind it.
page notice-login intranet.northwind.example/notices/sign-in
  title Northwind Intranet: sign in
  brand Northwind Intranet
  favorite
  heading Sign in to the Notice Board
  | Welcome back. Sign in to send staff notices.
  field Staff ID = dana.okafor
  field secret Password = Cedar-2026!
  button Sign in
page notice5-sent intranet.northwind.example/notices/sent/n5
  title Notice sent
  brand Northwind Intranet
  heading Notice sent
  | Network maintenance on Saturday: sent to all 42 staff.
page notice5-held intranet.northwind.example/notices/held/n5
  title Notice held
  brand Northwind Intranet
  heading Notice held
  | Network maintenance on Saturday: held. Marcus is told.
page notice-5 intranet.northwind.example/notices/n5
  title Send notice: network maintenance
  brand Northwind Intranet
  requires notice-login
  heading Send to all staff: network maintenance
  section
  row Written by = Marcus Lee, IT
  row Checked by = Nadia Khan, IT manager
  section
  | The Wi-Fi and the shared drive will be off from 7am to 9am on Saturday 14 November, while IT replaces a switch. Please save your work on Friday.
  button Send to all staff -> notice5-sent
  button Hold and ask IT -> notice5-held

mail mail-n5
  from Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject Notice for all staff: network maintenance Saturday
  link notice-5
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | Please send this notice to everyone. The Wi-Fi is off for two hours on Saturday morning. The link opens it on the Notice Board.
  |
  | Marcus, IT

step Notice 5 of 6
  look Mail: Marcus's message with the notice link
  | A new notice has come in from Marcus, in IT. It is a routine one. Send it the normal way, so you know what a real notice looks like.
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | Notice for all staff: network maintenance Saturday

step The link goes to the Notice Board
  look the link under Marcus's message
  when mail mail-n5
  | The email links to the company's own address, intranet.northwind.example, about something IT really plans. Hover over a link before you click it, to check where it goes.
open mail-n5

step Sign in as usual
  look the two boxes: Staff ID and Password, as on every visit
  when visit notice-5
  move Copy the details from intranet-login.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The Notice Board asks you to sign in first. Two boxes: Staff ID and Password. Remember what this page normally looks like.
open notice-5

step Check, then send
  look the first box: who wrote the notice, and who checked it
  when choose notice-login 1
  | You are in. Every notice shows who wrote it and who checked it. Marcus wrote this one and Nadia Khan, IT manager, checked it, so it is safe to send.
signin notice-login

step 5 of 6 done
  look the counter in the guide: one notice left
  when choose notice-5 any
  | Done. One notice to go. And another email has just arrived, from Sam in Sales.
choose notice-5 1
quota +1

# The lure: the warning itself, brought to the Notice Board by a worried colleague.
page hoax-sent intranet.northwind.example/notices/sent/n6
  title Notice sent
  brand Northwind Intranet
  heading Notice sent
  | Virus alert: sent to all 42 staff.
page hoax-held intranet.northwind.example/notices/held/n6
  title Notice held
  brand Northwind Intranet
  heading Notice held
  | Virus alert: held. IT will look at it and tell Sam.
page hoax-notice intranet.northwind.example/notices/n6
  title Send notice: virus alert
  brand Northwind Intranet
  requires notice-login
  heading Send to all staff: virus alert
  section
  row Written by = Unknown (forwarded email, posted by Sam Patel)
  row Checked by = Nobody
  section
  | WARNING! Do NOT read any email called "Good Times": it erases your whole disk, and your processor will be put into an nth-complexity infinite binary loop that can damage it. Experts confirm it. Send this to everyone!
  button Send to all staff -> hoax-sent
  button Hold and ask IT -> hoax-held
mail warning
  from Sam Patel <sam.patel@northwind.example>
  subject Fwd: Fwd: VIRUS ALERT: Do NOT read any email called "Good Times"
  link hoax-notice
  | Dana, can you send this to everyone? A friend sent it to me and it sounds serious. I have put it on the Notice Board for you.
  |
  | ---------- Forwarded message ----------
  | WARNING! Please read this and pass it on to everyone you know.
  |
  | A new virus is going round. If you get an email called "Good Times", do NOT open it. It will erase everything on your hard disk just by being read, and your processor will be put into an nth-complexity infinite binary loop that can damage it. A big computer company and a government agency have confirmed it.
  |
  | Forward this to everyone you can, right away!
  |
  | Sam
notify mail Sam Patel
  | Fwd: Fwd: VIRUS ALERT: Do NOT read any email called "Good Times"

step A frightening warning
  look the subject (Fwd: Fwd:), the forwarded part, and the last line: "Forward this to everyone"
  when mail warning
  | Sam, in Sales, has passed on a virus warning. It sounds urgent and official, and it asks Dana to send it to all staff. Read it all the way down, and flag anything that looks wrong.
open warning

step The same Notice Board
  look the first box: who wrote the notice, and who checked it
  when visit hoax-notice
  move Press “Send to all staff”, as the warning begs, to see how a hoax spreads. (At a real desk, “Hold and ask IT” is the safe choice.)
  | Sam has put the warning on the Notice Board, in the same form as Marcus's notice. Compare its first box with the one from a few minutes ago.
open hoax-notice

# What the hoax does: nothing to the computers, everything to the people.
step Sent to all 42 staff
  look the Notice sent page, then the copy in Mail's sent folder
  when choose hoax-notice 1
  | Dana sends it. Forty-two people now have a warning nobody can check, and it asks every one of them to pass it on.
choose hoax-notice 1
mail sent-notice
  folder sent
  from Dana Okafor <dana.okafor@northwind.example>
  to Northwind All Staff <all-staff@northwind.example>
  subject Staff notice: virus alert
  | WARNING! Do NOT read any email called "Good Times": it erases your whole disk, and your processor will be put into an nth-complexity infinite binary loop that can damage it. Experts confirm it. Send this to everyone!
mail panic-priya
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Re: VIRUS ALERT: is this true?
  | Dana, is this real? Should I switch my computer off? I have already sent it to my sister and to the whole Accounts team, just in case.
  |
  | Priya
mail panic-aisha
  from Aisha Bello <aisha.bello@northwind.example>
  subject Re: VIRUS ALERT
  | I have deleted every email called Good Times in my inbox, and I am not opening any mail today. Did I lose anything by doing that? Please tell me what to do.
  |
  | Aisha, Warehouse
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Re: VIRUS ALERT: is this true?

# The real "Good Times" message arrives in the middle of the panic.
step Everyone is frightened
  look Mail: the replies pouring in
  when mail panic-priya
  move Open the message called “Good Times” and read it.
  | Within minutes the replies start. Priya has passed it to her family and Accounts. Aisha deleted every email she had called Good Times. Nobody has checked if it is true.
  | And now a real email called "Good Times" has arrived.
open panic-priya
mail goodtimes
  from Tom Reyes <tom.reyes@northwind.example>
  subject Good Times
  | Hi all, Friday drinks at the Anchor are on at 6. See you there!
  |
  | Tom, Warehouse
notify mail Tom Reyes
  | Good Times

# IT answers. The hoax reference is defined first so IT's mail can link to it.
step An email called Good Times
  look the message: it is only a note about Friday drinks. Then Documents: every file is still there
  when mail goodtimes
  move Open Marcus's message about the warning and click its link.
  | It is from Tom, in the warehouse, and it is about Friday drinks. Dana read it, and nothing happened: all her files in Documents are still there.
  | A plain email is only text. It cannot run a program or erase a disk.
open goodtimes
open Documents
page hoaxbook hoax-reference.example/good-times
  title Hoax reference: "Good Times"
  heading Good Times virus warning
  brand Hoax Reference
  | Verdict: hoax. No virus called "Good Times" has ever existed.
  | Reading a plain-text email cannot run a program or erase a disk.
  section How to spot this kind of hoax
  item It warns of a terrible virus that works just by reading a message.
  item It says a famous company or agency has confirmed it, with no way to check.
  item It claims a harm no plain email can do, such as a processor put into a loop.
  item It ends with "send this to everyone you know".
  note The warning itself is the thing that spreads. Do not forward it.
mail it-note
  from Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject About the "Good Times" warning
  link hoaxbook
  | Dana, I have had a lot of calls about the "Good Times" warning. It is a hoax. You cannot catch a virus by reading plain email.
  |
  | There is a page that explains how to spot this kind of hoax. Please do not send it on, and send any virus warning to me first.
  |
  | Marcus, IT
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | About the "Good Times" warning

step The hoax reference
  look the verdict "hoax" and the list of signs
  when visit hoaxbook
  move Open Tomas's message and click its link.
  | Marcus's link goes to a hoax reference page. It says plainly that this is a hoax, and it lists the signs of one.
  | Sam's warning had all four of them.
open hoaxbook

# The last real notice.
page notice7-sent intranet.northwind.example/notices/sent/n7
  title Notice sent
  brand Northwind Intranet
  heading Notice sent
  | Fire drill on Tuesday: sent to all 42 staff.
page notice7-held intranet.northwind.example/notices/held/n7
  title Notice held
  brand Northwind Intranet
  heading Notice held
  | Fire drill on Tuesday: held. Tomas is told.
page notice-6 intranet.northwind.example/notices/n7
  title Send notice: fire drill
  brand Northwind Intranet
  requires notice-login
  heading Send to all staff: fire drill
  section
  row Written by = Tomas Rivera, Facilities
  row Checked by = Nadia Khan, Operations
  section
  | There will be a fire drill on Tuesday 17 November at 10:30am. Please leave by the nearest exit when the alarm sounds and meet at the car park.
  button Send to all staff -> notice7-sent
  button Hold and ask IT -> notice7-held
mail mail-n6
  from Tomas Rivera <tomas.rivera@northwind.example>
  subject Notice for all staff: fire drill on Tuesday
  link notice-6
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | The fire drill notice is on the Notice Board, ready to send. The link opens it.
  |
  | Tomas, Facilities
notify mail Tomas Rivera
  | Notice for all staff: fire drill on Tuesday

step Back to work
  look Mail: Tomas's message, then the first box on the notice: who wrote it and who checked it
  when visit notice-6
  | The last notice is an ordinary one from Tomas, in Facilities. Its first box names who wrote it and who checked it, like Marcus's did. Check it, then send it.
open notice-6

step 6 of 6 done
  look the counter: the shift is done
  when choose notice-6 any
  | Done. Six of six notices are out. But Aisha has replied, and she has not read the fire drill notice.
choose notice-6 1
quota +1
mail aisha-reply
  from Aisha Bello <aisha.bello@northwind.example>
  subject Re: Notice for all staff: fire drill on Tuesday
  | Dana, is this notice the virus? I am not opening mail today, so I have not read it. Is the fire drill real?
  |
  | Aisha, Warehouse
notify mail Aisha Bello
  | Re: Notice for all staff: fire drill on Tuesday

step What the hoax did
  look the animation: a warning that spread by itself
  | No virus touched a single computer. The hoax spread because kind, worried people did what it asked. It cost the office a morning of calls, deleted mail, and a fire drill that people did not read.
attack mass-mail-worm
  | to all 42 staff
  | forwarded to families and friends
  | 61 calls and messages to IT
  | no virus anywhere
  | the fire drill notice went unread
mail helpdesk
  from Northwind IT <servicedesk@northwind.example>
  subject Helpdesk: 61 messages about one hoax
  | This morning the service desk took 61 calls and messages about the "Good Times" warning. Nobody had a virus. Three people deleted real mail by mistake, and the fire drill notice went unread.
  |
  | Northwind IT
notify mail Northwind IT
  | Helpdesk: 61 messages about one hoax

flag hard mail warning
  | A frightening warning that asks to be sent to everyone, with no name and nothing to check. That is how a chain hoax is built.
flag hard section hoax-notice 1
  label Who wrote it, and who checked it
  | The first box says it all: the writer is unknown, it is only a forwarded email, and nobody checked it. Marcus's notice named who wrote it and who checked it.
flag hard section hoax-notice 2
  label The text of the warning
  | An email cannot erase a disk or damage a processor just by being read, and the "experts" have no names, so nobody can check them.
flag hard choice hoax-notice 1
  | Sending it to everyone is what the hoax wants: it lives only as long as people pass it on.
flag hard mail goodtimes
  | The "Good Times" email, read at last: a note about Friday drinks. Nothing happened, because nothing could.
flag hard file Documents/Q3-report.docx
  label Q3-report.docx in Documents
  | Still there after the "Good Times" email was read. Reading an email erased nothing.
flag hard mail panic-aisha
  | A chain message got a colleague to delete her mail and stop opening it. That is the harm a hoax really does.
flag soft file Desktop/intranet-login.txt
  | Passwords in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read them. Use a password manager.

lesson
  | A virus warning that says "send this to everyone" is the hoax, even when a colleague sends it in good faith. Search the title on a hoax-reference site before you forward anything.
  | A plain email cannot erase a disk by being read. Do not delete files, switch off the computer or stop opening mail because a chain message says so.
  | Send virus warnings to IT, not to your friends or to the whole company: real alerts come from your security software or IT, not chain emails.
